<h1>Hardware Settings - Sensor Tab</h1>
<hr align="left" width="100.526%" size="5" class="whs3">
<p class="description"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Description: Sensor</b></span> Tab changes options related to the sensor settings.</p>
<p><img src="../images/hw_sensor.gif" width="551px" height="485px" border="0" class="img_whs4" alt=""></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB">The tab is divided in four sections and a checkbox for 'Certified settings':</span></p>
<p class="sub-head">Profiles <a href="Profiles_in_HW_settings_(MMD).htm"><img src="../images/i_blue_sm.jpg" width="23px" height="16px" border="0" class="img_whs5" alt=""></a></p>
<p class="parameterdescription">&#160;</p>
<p class="sub-head"><span style="text-decoration:none;">L</span>aser Power section</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">Click and drag the <span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>'Laser Power'</b></span> indicator to the desired setting or type-in the percentage in the box right to the slider. (0 is the minimum, 100 the maximum) The default value is 25%.</p>
<p id="p1" class="Note"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Note</b></span>: For MM D sensors 'Laser power' slider means percentage of the allowed range of the laser power. For example, if laser power is restricted to level 2 then value of 25 means 25% of 2, which is level 0.5.</p>
<p id="p1">&#160;</p>
<p class="sub-head"><span style="text-decoration:none;">Ambient Threshold section</span></p>
<p class="parameterstitle">Value edit box</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">This box contains the ambient threshold setting of the current material item option. Sets the sensitivity of the sensor to surrounding light. A high value is used in a bright environment, a lower value in a darker environment. Lower values can be better at picking up darker colours. Default value is 96.</p>
<p class="parameterstitle">Threshold filling</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">If enabled this option allows achieving better results when scanning dark surfaces and very shiny surfaces with reflections. The appropriate laser power and threshold values must be used in these cases. The option is available for MMD sensors only.</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">&#160;</p>
<p class="sub-head">Info section</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">Displays versions of the built-in software for controlling the sensor.</p>
<p class="parameterstitle">Sets system triggering.</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">If <span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Best for localiser</b></span> option is checked then KUBE sets system triggering according to the current localiser that is installed. For FARO and CimCore localisers system triggering is disabled, for K-Scan it is enabled. If <span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Best for localiser</b></span> option is unchecked then user could manually select whether to use system triggering or not. It is strongly recommended to enable <span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Best for localiser</b></span> option in order to use the proper triggering.</p>
<p>&#160;</p>
<p class="sub-head">Sensor section</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">Sensor type, Sensor serial number, Date of manufacture, and Temperature (internal temperature of the sensor) will be automatically detected by the software for D sensors.</p>
<p>&#160;</p>
<p class="sub-head">Sensor detection section</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">If <span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Auto</b></span> is selected then KUBE tries detecting all types of sensors until it finds the installed one. If set to <span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Manual</b></span> then KUBE tries to detect only the sensor type, which is selected in the listbox. In case of fault it doesn't detect other types. Currently available options are 'MM X/Y', 'MM Z', 'MM D', and 'None'.</p>
<p>&#160;</p>
<p class="sub-head">Refresh MMIB/Sensor status button</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">This button is used to refresh the settings if a sensor is changed. This includes MMIB/sensor detection and loading the corresponding calibration file for the detected sensor.</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">&#160;</p>
<p class="sub-head">Advanced Settings dialog</p>
<p>The settings in this dialog depend on the arm connected to the PC. Here below follows the full description of this dialog, some notes are added for each arm type.</p>
<p class="whs6"><img src="../images/D_Hardware_Sensor_Advanced_Settings.gif" width="525px" height="459px" border="0" class="img_whs7" alt=""></p>
<p class="parameterstitle">Laser standoff indicator</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">Controls the intensity level of spot laser standoff indicator.</p>
<ul>
<li class="kadov-p-Cbullet-circle">
<p class="bullet-circle"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>'Auto'</b></span> - in this mode the intensity of the spot laser automatically follows changes of the intensity of the laser stripe. This is the default option.</p>
</li>
<li class="kadov-p-Cbullet-circle">
<p class="bullet-circle"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>'%'</b></span> - this option allows manual adjustment of spot laser intensity. Default value is 33% of the intensity of the laser stripe.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p class="parameterstitle">Dynamic threshold</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">When this option is enabled MM D uses enhanced method for distinguishing real stripe points from the noise in the image based on percentage from the maximum intensity found in the image line. Disabled by default.</p>
<p class="parameterstitle">Data Rate</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">This group contains set of options allowing user to control the actual rate of incoming stripe data by limiting the number of points in captured images. Scan rate (frames per second) is automatically calculated depending on exposure time and the options below.</p>
<ul>
<li class="kadov-p-Cbullet-circle">
<p class="bullet-circle"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Y -</b></span> This group contains options that control the number of captured lines in the image, which corresponds to the number of points along the stripe (Y direction). The number of points per stripe is automatically calculated depending on the options in this group.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p class="whs8"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>- '100%'</b></span> and <span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>'50%'</b></span> options - percentage of points that will be used. 100% means full size and 50% means half size. The default option is 100%.</p>
<p class="whs8"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>- 'Sample 1/2'</b></span> checkbox - this option allows further to lower number of captured lines in the image by factor of two by taking each second line. Disabled by default.</p>
<ul>
<li class="kadov-p-Cbullet-circle">
<p class="bullet-circle"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Z -</b></span> This group contains options that control the number of captured rows in the image, which corresponds to active area of the sensor in depth (Z direction). The default option is 'Default'.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p class="whs9">- <span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>'100% (... mm)'</b></span> - means full size of the range.</p>
<p class="whs9">- <span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>'77% (... mm)'</b></span> - means default size of the range that is optimal combination of FOV and scanning rate.</p>
<p class="whs9">- <span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>'50% (... mm)'</b></span> - means half size of the range.</p>
<p class="whs9">- <span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>'24% (... mm)'</b></span> - means quarter size of the Z range allowing scanning at the maximum number of 80000 points per second. In this mode Z range is very limited and points are captured when laser line is positioned where the spot point is.</p>
<p class="whs9">The size of FOV depends on the sensor model and current sensor calibration.</p>
<p class="whs9">The available combinations for Y and Z ranges + different localiser types are:</p>
